WEBVTT

00:00:00.288 --> 00:00:01.628
Welcome to This Explainer.

00:00:01.628 --> 00:00:05.708
Today, we are demystifying how complex
data is organized behind the scenes.

00:00:05.708 --> 00:00:09.228
By building a foundational digital
structure completely from scratch,

00:00:09.228 --> 00:00:12.268
let's jump right into lesson
02, Understanding Nodes.

00:00:13.152 --> 00:00:15.592
Here's our roadmap. One, what is a node?

00:00:16.224 --> 00:00:18.048
Two, left and right children.

00:00:18.432 --> 00:00:20.884
Three, leaves and node roles.

00:00:20.884 --> 00:00:24.000
And four, how nodes form
trees. Let's get to it.

00:00:24.864 --> 00:00:26.764
What exactly is a node?

00:00:27.552 --> 00:00:30.492
Well, you can think of a
node as a labeled container.

00:00:30.492 --> 00:00:34.652
Inside, you store your actual data
value, like a number or a name.

00:00:34.652 --> 00:00:37.532
But the real magic? That
is in the references.

00:00:37.532 --> 00:00:40.012
These act like built-in
physical addresses,

00:00:40.012 --> 00:00:43.692
telling the computer exactly where the
next connected nodes live in its memory.

00:00:44.544 --> 00:00:47.004
Take a look at this. It's
the perfect starting point.

00:00:47.004 --> 00:00:50.164
A single isolated node
holding the number 42.

00:00:50.164 --> 00:00:52.044
It's doing a great job holding that data,

00:00:52.044 --> 00:00:54.244
but it doesn't connect
to anything just yet.

00:00:54.244 --> 00:00:56.804
It is literally the purest
form of our building block,

00:00:56.804 --> 00:00:58.272
just waiting for instructions.

00:00:58.848 --> 00:00:59.748
Left and right children.

00:01:00.672 --> 00:01:04.692
Now, in a binary tree, a node has
a maximum of two reference slots,

00:01:04.692 --> 00:01:07.172
specifically a left
child and a right child.

00:01:07.172 --> 00:01:09.412
You can think of them as connection ports.

00:01:09.412 --> 00:01:13.132
But what if a node doesn't have
anything to connect to on one side?

00:01:13.132 --> 00:01:16.652
If a child slot is empty, its
reference is set to nothing.

00:01:17.184 --> 00:01:19.564
In the programming world,
we call that absence null.

00:01:20.256 --> 00:01:22.876
So, check out how this
hierarchy starts to form.

00:01:22.876 --> 00:01:25.716
The parent node, 50,
sits right at the top.

00:01:25.716 --> 00:01:29.796
Its left reference actively points
down to a new node containing 30,

00:01:29.796 --> 00:01:33.076
and its right reference points
over to a node containing 70.

00:01:33.076 --> 00:01:36.996
Just like that, our single box has
transformed into a tiny, connected network.

00:01:38.016 --> 00:01:39.396
leaves and node rolls.

00:01:40.224 --> 00:01:43.164
You can figure out a node's role
just by counting its children.

00:01:43.164 --> 00:01:44.352
It's super straightforward.

00:01:44.736 --> 00:01:47.804
Zero children, that's a dead
end, so we call it leaf.

00:01:47.804 --> 00:01:52.044
Exactly one child, that node acts as
a link, continuing a single chain.

00:01:52.608 --> 00:01:55.788
And if it has two children, like
that number 50 we just looked at,

00:01:55.788 --> 00:01:59.388
it's a branch port, splitting the path
in two completely different directions.

00:02:00.288 --> 00:02:03.128
Looking at this larger map,
those roles are crystal clear.

00:02:03.128 --> 00:02:06.968
Node 15 is our branch point,
splitting off to 12 and 20.

00:02:07.584 --> 00:02:09.888
Node 5 just continues a single chain.

00:02:10.204 --> 00:02:16.128
And down at the absolute bottom, 2, 12,
and 20 are our dead ends, the leaves.

00:02:16.484 --> 00:02:18.884
And here's a highly
practical takeaway for you.

00:02:18.884 --> 00:02:22.964
In a binary search tree, those leaves are
exactly where new values are usually added

00:02:22.964 --> 00:02:23.964
when the tree needs to grow.

00:02:24.960 --> 00:02:28.300
And finally, section 4.
How nodes form trees.

00:02:29.184 --> 00:02:32.204
Every journey through this structure
has to start somewhere, right?

00:02:32.204 --> 00:02:35.404
That primary entrance is
always called the root node.

00:02:35.404 --> 00:02:37.724
The root is basically the ultimate parent.

00:02:37.724 --> 00:02:41.164
If you know where the root is, you hold
the master key to the whole structure,

00:02:41.164 --> 00:02:43.059
because you can simply
follow those built-in

00:02:43.059 --> 00:02:45.084
references down through
the branches and chains

00:02:45.084 --> 00:02:48.192
to reach absolutely every
other node in the entire tree.

00:02:48.960 --> 00:02:53.000
Now, because these references only
flow downward, there is a golden rule.

00:02:53.000 --> 00:02:55.090
You will only ever find one exact path

00:02:55.090 --> 00:02:57.345
connecting the root to any other specific

00:02:57.345 --> 00:02:57.620
node.

00:02:58.368 --> 00:02:59.328
Think about that for a second.

00:02:59.708 --> 00:03:02.097
Unlike a messy city map
where you can take five

00:03:02.097 --> 00:03:04.588
different detours to get
to the exact same place,

00:03:04.588 --> 00:03:09.024
a tree structure is perfectly decisive.
No looping back. No shortcuts.

00:03:09.328 --> 00:03:12.928
From the root to your target node,
there was only ever one true path.

00:03:13.632 --> 00:03:17.612
But that brings up a really
fascinating structural puzzle for us.

00:03:17.612 --> 00:03:21.892
What exactly happens if the address for
the root itself is completely missing?

00:03:22.560 --> 00:03:24.724
Imagine showing up to
the main entrance of our

00:03:24.724 --> 00:03:26.700
data structure, ready to follow the paths,

00:03:26.700 --> 00:03:29.568
only to find the starting
reference is entirely empty.

00:03:29.952 --> 00:03:30.912
Just a null value.

00:03:31.392 --> 00:03:32.736
What does that mean for
the rest of the tree?

00:03:33.408 --> 00:03:38.148
Well, in that specific scenario, you
simply have what's known as an empty tree.

00:03:38.148 --> 00:03:40.768
It's a structure with
absolutely no nodes at all,

00:03:40.768 --> 00:03:43.228
just waiting for its very
first piece of data.

00:03:43.228 --> 00:03:44.868
It's completely blank canvas.

00:03:45.792 --> 00:03:47.808
So, here's a final puzzle to ponder.

00:03:48.192 --> 00:03:50.412
If a tree is defined by its connections,

00:03:50.412 --> 00:03:52.952
but an empty tree has no nodes to connect,

00:03:52.952 --> 00:03:56.652
at what exact moment does data
stop being just a random value

00:03:56.652 --> 00:03:58.652
and actually become a structured tree?

00:03:59.136 --> 00:04:01.357
Think about that as you
consider how all digital

00:04:01.357 --> 00:04:03.116
information begins from absolute zero.

00:04:03.744 --> 00:04:06.364
Thanks for hanging out with me for
this explainer, and keep exploring!
